WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.630
AI already helps us write, translate, and
find information.

2
00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:07.730
Imagine a friendly assistant working beside
you.

3
00:00:07.805 --> 00:00:11.460
It can save time, but a fluent answer can
still be wrong.

4
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.230
Trust means being willing to rely on
something.

5
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.105
Warranted trust means having good reasons
for that reliance.

6
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:28.080
The evidence we need changes when an answer
affects our health, our rights, or our
security.

7
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:35.840
The European approach connects three
foundations: lawful, ethical, and robust.

8
00:00:35.915 --> 00:00:40.540
AI should follow laws, respect human rights,
and work reliably.

9
00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:44.440
Seven dimensions turn these principles into
practical questions.

10
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.880
Human agency and oversight keep people
involved in meaningful ways.

11
00:00:49.955 --> 00:00:54.605
In a clinic, an AI suggestion should support
the professional’s judgment.

12
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.530
People need clear limits, opportunities to
intervene, and a path to help.

13
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:07.480
Technical robustness and safety ask how
systems handle mistakes, attacks, and
unfamiliar situations.

14
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:14.805
At an identity check, a detector must face
changing deepfakes while avoiding
unnecessary rejection of real people.

15
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:21.305
Privacy and data governance concern how
information is collected, protected, and
used.

16
00:01:21.355 --> 00:01:25.005
Our assistant should not expose a student’s
personal details.

17
00:01:25.105 --> 00:01:29.780
Good evaluation also asks whether the data
are appropriate and representative.

18
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:35.880
Transparency includes explainability:
understanding why an AI produced its result.

19
00:01:35.955 --> 00:01:40.230
Explainable AI, or X A I, provides tools for
this.

20
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.730
We must check that the explanation reflects
the system and helps its users.

21
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.130
Fairness asks who receives the benefits and
who experiences the errors.

22
00:01:50.205 --> 00:01:53.480
A skin classifier may work differently
across groups.

23
00:01:53.555 --> 00:01:58.280
Test relevant populations, including people
missing from the training data.

24
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.605
AI also affects communities and the
environment.

25
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.580
Consider access, jobs, energy, and resource
use.

26
00:02:07.655 --> 00:02:14.630
A useful service should be evaluated beyond
its immediate users, including people
affected indirectly by its decisions.

27
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:22.705
Accountability keeps responsibility clear:
who develops the system, checks it, and
responds when something goes wrong?

28
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.580
People need ways to question decisions and
seek a remedy.

29
00:02:26.655 --> 00:02:29.780
A human organization must remain answerable.

30
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.705
How do we put these questions into practice?

31
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:39.680
Evaluate the system for its intended use,
document the evidence and its limits, and
monitor what changes.

32
00:02:39.755 --> 00:02:43.530
Then improve the model, data, or surrounding
process.

33
00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:51.570
Return to our assistant: useful AI deserves
careful questions, not automatic belief.

34
00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.920
For each task, ask what evidence would
justify relying on it.

35
00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.595
Warranted trust grows from evidence and
responsibility.
